minisign — third-party attribution

This file carries a CONDITION. `src/root.zig`'s `isPrintableComment` is a port
of `is_printable` from jedisct1/minisign, and the ISC license that project
ships under requires its copyright notice and permission text to be retained.
That text is reproduced in full below, and root NOTICE §1 lists this file.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, 2026-08-14. Until this revision the module argued merger
doctrine instead: a thirty-line control-character predicate is small and
precisely specified, so the argument ran, it has no other reasonable
expression and nothing is owed. That argument may well hold. It is not the one
this repository acts on, for two reasons. The module's own doc comment calls
the function a "Faithful port of minisign.c's `is_printable`", and a file that
argues no-attribution while the code it describes says "port" is a
contradiction a reader has to resolve. And the precedent cuts the other way:
`poseidon` carries an attribution file for porting a sage script of comparable
size. Consistency is cheaper than defending the distinction, and ISC's
condition — retain the notice — costs a consumer nothing.

Required attribution — minisign `is_printable`
----------------------------------------------

Ported: ONE function. `isPrintableComment` in `src/root.zig` is
`minisign.c`'s `is_printable` translated to Zig: the ASCII tab / 0x20-0x7E
fast path, the 2/3/4-byte UTF-8 length classification, the continuation-byte
and overlong/surrogate range checks, and the final rejection of code points in
the C0 and C1 control ranges. It is a port, not a description of behaviour
re-implemented — the Zig retains a check for a zero continuation byte, which
is only meaningful against a NUL-terminated C string and is dead on a Zig
slice. It exists to stop a maliciously-crafted trusted comment from smuggling
terminal escape sequences into a verifier's output.

Nothing else in this module is ported, and no minisign C source is otherwise
present; see the provenance section below for what the rest rests on.

